1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,225
Every design meeting needs a diagram.

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:05,363
Twenty minutes of boxes and arrows.

3
00:00:05,550 --> 00:00:07,129
Five minutes of explaining.

4
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:09,927
Draft Canvas was built to flip that.

5
00:00:11,333 --> 00:00:12,933
The arrows label themselves.

6
00:00:16,753 --> 00:00:17,841
Draw it yourself…

7
00:00:18,153 --> 00:00:20,308
or hand the pencil to your coding agent.

8
00:00:20,803 --> 00:00:24,344
It reads your code and draws the
architecture, pass by pass.

9
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:29,245
Ask for a cleaner layout — it fixes that
too.

10
00:00:29,450 --> 00:00:31,754
Now, the part that matters: explaining it.

11
00:00:31,900 --> 00:00:36,572
Start with the whole system, then step
inside any service, down to its components.

12
00:00:36,750 --> 00:00:38,563
Present the flow, step by step.

13
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:42,543
Work on it together, live.

14
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:45,153
Take it anywhere — even draw.io.

15
00:00:45,990 --> 00:00:46,950
No account.

16
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:48,123
No tracking.

17
00:00:48,670 --> 00:00:49,950
Saved on your device.

18
00:00:50,620 --> 00:00:52,071
Free and open source.

19
00:00:52,250 --> 00:00:57,583
So, the next design meeting: five minutes of
boxes and arrows — and twenty of explaining!

20
00:00:58,066 --> 00:00:59,581
Draw less. Explain more.
